    <!-- About -->
    <string name="about_feature_1">Real-time telemetry of 12 core charge/discharge metrics: health / remaining charge / current capacity / design capacity / percentage / battery status / temperature / health condition / voltage / cycle count / instant current / full-charge capacity</string>
    <string name="about_feature_2">Full-charge detection: when charge reaches 100% and instant current falls into the 0-25 mA float cutoff band, the measured full-charge capacity is locked automatically and saved across sessions</string>
    <string name="about_feature_3">Hardware lab: one-tap scenario simulation (full-charge calibration / flash charge / gaming discharge / overheat alert) with six tuning sliders and one-tap restore of real hardware telemetry</string>
    <string name="about_feature_4">Hardware parsing: five-field SoC profile (model / vendor / process / CPU / GPU) plus PMIC model, fast-charge protocol and cell structure via three-level fallback detection</string>
    <string name="about_feature_5">Data sources: system BatteryManager public APIs, the ACTION_BATTERY_CHANGED sticky broadcast and real sysfs hardware nodes. All computed locally, nothing is uploaded</string>
    <string name="about_feature_6">Permissions: no runtime or dangerous permissions requested; all features work right after install (the merged manifest only contains signature-level internal permissions injected by the system framework, with no privacy impact)</string>
    <string name="about_title">About</string>
    <string name="about_tagline">Battery Master — Professional Android battery charge/discharge monitor</string>
    <string name="about_privacy_title">Privacy &amp; Data Statement</string>
    <string name="about_privacy_body">Zero permissions: no runtime/dangerous permissions requested; battery data comes entirely from public system APIs and sysfs hardware nodes. Zero uploads: all metrics, telemetry history and sessions are stored only in app-private storage with no network transfer. Zero tracking: no third-party analytics or crash SDKs; crash stacks are stored locally and leave the device only when you export them. Uninstalling removes all data.</string>
    <string name="about_export_crash_log">Export crash logs</string>
    <string name="about_crash_count">%1$d crash stacks stored locally</string>
    <string name="about_crash_none">No local crash records</string>
    <string name="about_developer">Developer: 某厂大牛马【带娃的小陈工】</string>

